Original commit body from d0da67b2d673b4d1a4ea3b798daa5edf195a9a48:

Two fixes to the same root problem: the gate could not say what it did
not cover, and the comment that appeared to explain its boundary was
wrong.

THE FALSE CLAIM. The matrix carried:

    All eight are availability="core" (no grammar wheel needed).

It was load-bearing -- read as the REASON the gate stops at eight
languages, making any extension look like it would drag in optional
dependencies. Measured against the live catalog, only `python` is
availability="core" (stdlib `ast`); java, javascript, go, rust, csharp
and swift are all "extra" -- the SAME tier as the scala, kotlin and cpp
analyzers this matrix does NOT gate, whose grammars are hard
dependencies of hypergumbo-lang-mainstream and install wherever it does.

Two unrelated senses of "core" had collided: the catalog's
`availability="core"` means "needs no extra dependency"; this file's
prose meant "the important ones". The collision manufactured a
dependency barrier that does not exist. The real boundary is 7 of 38
mainstream analyzers, chosen historically and never argued for in an ADR.

THE DECLARED SCOPE. Three maps plus a test that they exhaust the tier:

  * FIXTURE_ANALYZER      -- the 7 gated analyzers (8 fixture rows)
  * UNGATED_CODE_LANGUAGES -- 14 mainstream code languages this gate says
    nothing about (php, ruby, scala, kotlin, cpp, groovy, objc, c, bash,
    lua, perl, powershell, sql, jupyter). Real coverage gaps, not
    exemptions.
  * NON_CONSTRUCT_FORMATS  -- 17 config/data/markup/build formats with no
    class-or-callable surface for the uniform construct set to be uniform
    ABOUT. A row made almost entirely of COLUMN_APPLICABILITY exemptions
    is where a real gap hides behind a plausible excuse, which is already
    visible in `entrypoint_concept` applying to 1 of 8 languages.

`test_matrix_scope_is_declared_and_exhaustive` fails when a mainstream
analyzer appears in none of the three, so analyzer #39 forces a one-line
classification at the cheapest possible moment; it also fails on a stale
entry naming a deleted analyzer, and on bucket overlap. Both directions
were mutation-tested rather than assumed -- removing `ruby` and adding a
nonexistent `kobol` each produce the intended failure with an actionable
message.

WHY THIS IS MORE THAN BOOKKEEPING. An empty KNOWN_HOLES reads as "the
fleet is at parity"; it means "every GATED cell is a hard lock" -- the
standing lesson that a gate's "0 violations" is bounded by what its
fixtures execute, and that reporting it unscoped overstates even when
every number is accurate. The spec already models the honest alternative:
it reports identity coverage as "~41 of ~70" and delegates to ADR-0014's
census rather than claiming completeness. The module docstring now states
the scope the same way, and `test_gated_share_of_mainstream_is_disclosed`
fails if that disclosure is ever deleted.

No behavior change; test and docs only. The question of whether the
boundary SHOULD move is deliberately left open -- this makes it visible,
not settled. WI-kajip proposes the fixture-free alternative (a
container-membership validator class under ADR-0033, which sees
contains edges and so catches both the analyzer-side and pipeline-side
failure modes WI-duguk needed two separate fixes for); WI-dorop records
three measured defects already sitting in the ungated set.

